SkinProfile:
  Name: Reference Head
  # TRANSPORT VERSION 3 (issue #1394) — LayeredSpecular: #1241's diffusion,
  # #1242's thin-region transmission and #1243's layered specular. The version
  # is the AUTHORING switch, not the material's kind and not its PBR closure
  # version; see docs/adr/0024-material-kind-is-not-the-closure-version.md.
  #
  # WHY 3 AND NOT HIGHER. 4 adds a wet oral coat and 5 a cornea — both neutral
  # by default, and a cranium is neither a mouth nor an eye. 6 changes the SHAPE
  # of the diffusion (the isotropic gather, #1368) and is deliberately NOT
  # neutral on arrival; moving there is its own decision with its own rebake,
  # not part of switching transmission and layered specular on.
  #
  # Every field below the diffusion block is AUTHORED ON. The neutral values —
  # Transmission.Strength 0 and the four Specular fields at 0 — render the
  # version-1 frame exactly (SkinReferenceHeadEvidenceTest's neutral arm), so a
  # version bump with those would have moved the number and changed nothing.
  EvaluationModel: 3
  Units: "colours: linear Rec.709 (unitless 0..1); lengths: millimetres"
  # Transport albedo: the fraction of light entering the surface that leaves it
  # again rather than being absorbed. Red survives, blue does not.
  ScatterColor: [0.85, 0.55, 0.45]
  # Per-channel mean free path in MILLIMETRES. A mid-tone dermis: red travels
  # roughly 1.5 mm, blue barely leaves the epidermis. Read by the diffusion
  # (Renderer/SkinDiffusion.h) AND by the transmission below — the same
  # per-channel reach decides how far light spreads and how much crosses an ear,
  # which is what makes a backlit ear red rather than white.
  ScatterRadiusMM: [1.55, 0.8, 0.55]
  # Takes the material's authored thickness (metres, per glTF KHR_materials_volume)
  # into the millimetre space of the radii above. 1000 is the identity.
  ThicknessScale: 1000
  # Multiplies the SURFACE specular term. Slightly warm and slightly below one:
  # skin's oil layer is not a perfect white mirror.
  SpecularTint: [0.97, 0.93, 0.9]
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # THIN-REGION TRANSMISSION (issue #1242). How much light crossing a thin
  # region of this head leaves toward the viewer. HOW MUCH crosses is not here:
  # it is exp(-thickness / d) per channel, from the material's thickness and the
  # radii above. These three shape the EXIT only.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Transmission:
    # 1 — the physical answer. The term is bounded by the transmittance, which
    # is bounded by ScatterColor (SkinTransmission.h, premise 3); anything
    # below 1 would be a second, unphysical absorption on top of the one the
    # radii already describe.
    Strength: 1
    # The exit lobe's forward bias. Dermal tissue scatters strongly forward per
    # event (g ~ 0.8-0.9, Jacques 2013, "Optical properties of biological
    # tissues: a review"), and a few millimetres of ear is only a handful of
    # scattering lengths, so the light has not forgotten its direction:
    # 0.7 is that single-event bias partly washed out by the multiple
    # scattering, and it is the value docs/guides/skin-transmission.md gives
    # for an ear.
    Anisotropy: 0.7
    # The forward part's sharpness. 4: a soft forward glow that is brightest
    # looking into the light, not a specular spike (the ceiling, 64, is one).
    # The same as the oral and ocular profiles, so the head and its parts exit
    # alike.
    Power: 4
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # LAYERED SPECULAR (issue #1243). Values read off the in-repo measurement,
  # experiments/skin-specular-reference/compare_lobes.py, at the footprint this
  # head is seen at: DigitalHuman.olo frames it at ~0.30 mm per pixel (0.34 m
  # away, 45 degree vertical field, 942 px tall), and a close-up that fills a
  # 1080p frame is ~0.25 mm. That is the experiment's COARSE regime, 250-500 um,
  # where a pixel straddles oily and dry skin and the second lobe earns its place.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Specular:
    # w, the broad lobe's share. The fitted weight is 0.05-0.10 at fine
    # footprints and 0.55-0.60 from 250 um up; this head is seen at ~300 um.
    # (The same fit puts the NARROW lobe at ~0.3x the filtered alpha, i.e. it
    # wants a smoother base roughness than a single lobe does. That is the
    # MATERIAL's Roughness, not a profile field, and is left to the scenes.)
    LobeMix: 0.55
    # s, broad roughness / narrow roughness, on PERCEPTUAL roughness. The
    # experiment fits a broad/narrow ALPHA ratio of 3.88 at 250 um and 5.42 at
    # 500 um; this field is its square root, 1.97-2.33, so 2.
    LobeRoughnessScale: 2
    # sigma^2, the screen-space variance strength. Fitted 0.15 at 250 um and
    # 0.75 at 500 um. The scene head is ~13% under adult scale, so 0.30 mm per
    # pixel is ~0.34 mm of real skin; log-interpolated there, 0.3.
    NormalVarianceStrength: 0.3
    # 0 on both detail fields, and this is a measurement rather than a default:
    # neither subject that wears this profile binds a normal map (the scan
    # ships a BUMP map, not a normal map), so there is no pore band for a gain
    # to act on, and the expression gain additionally never reaches a
    # morph-only entity until #1395 lands.
    DetailStrength: 0
    ExpressionDetailGain: 0
